    {
      "title": "Mutually exclusive, independent, dependent events",
      "html": "<p><b>Mutually exclusive, independent, dependent events</b> is a key part of Probability (SSS 3, Mathematics). Master the meaning, the main points and how it is tested in JAMB and WAEC questions on this topic.</p><h4>Key points</h4><ul><li>If $A,B$ are mutually exclusive : $P(A\\cup B) = P(A) + P(B)$.</li><li>Multiplication law: If $A,B$ are independent : $P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B)$.</li><li>If dependent: $P(A\\cap B) = P(A)P(B|A)$.</li></ul><h4>Worked example</h4><p><b>Q:</b> If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, $P(A\\cup B) =$ ?<br/><b>A:</b> P(A)+P(B) \u2014 No overlap, so probabilities add.</p><p><b>Q:</b> If $A$ and $B$ are independent, $P(A\\cap B) =$ ?<br/><b>A:</b> P(A)P(B) \u2014 Independence means multiply.</p>",
      "examples": [
        {
          "q": "If $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, $P(A\\cup B) =$ ?",
